\begin_layout Section
Abstract
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Background: 
\series default
The Mega2 software program has been available since 1/16/2000 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mega2,_the_Manipulation_Environment_for_Genetic_Anal
ysis
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 It converts data from several different genetic analysis data formats into
 the specific data requirements for over 40 commonly used linkage analysis
 programs.
 Additional analysis programs have been added over time, but this has required
 software developers keenly knowledgeable in the Mega2 internals.
 The R community and Bioconductor community have developed a variety of
 genetic analysis programs as well.
 Typically, these programs and R interpreter, in general, perform poorly
 when faced with the large amount of raw genotype data arising from current
 GWAS analysis.
 Recently, Mega2 has been enhanced to use an SQLite database 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://www.sqlite.org/
\end_layout

\end_inset

 as an intermediate data representation.
 Further, bialleleic data are now stored in a highly compressed form, much
 like the GenABEL R facility and PLINK binary format.
 With these enhancements, it is now possible to read the data assembled
 by Mega2 into R data frames and continue processing with R and only decompressi
ng required genotype data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Results: 
\series default
We have made it possible to load SQLite3 Mega2 databases into R.
 Accessing data frames holding these data are very straight forward in R
 for whatever purpose the user needs.
 In addition, we have made C++ functions for R to decompress the genotype
 data that are available in the Mega2 database and return them as a simple
 matrix.
 Besides the base Mega2+R package that loads and manipulates data frames,
 we have also created several more R packages that illustrate data access
 as well as perform useful functions: one to run the Bioconductor pedgene
 package on selected marker subsets, one to output data frames data as a
 VCF file 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/GenABEL/index.html
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and related files (for phenotype and family data), and one to convert the
 data frames into GenABEL 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

http://www.genabel.org/
\end_layout

\end_inset

 R 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/GenABEL/index.html
\end_layout

\end_inset

 gwaa.data-class objects.
 (Note: The Mega2 program supports more input data formats than GenABEL
 (such as PLINK bed, VCF and IMPUTE2) and thus using Mega2+R allows these
 formats to be processed by GenABEL.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Conclusions: Mega2+R
\series default
 is a collection of R packages that lets users access the GWAS data read
 in by Mega2 and then to do subsequent manipulation of the data in R.
 We have found the results useful and wish to make them available to a wider
 audience.
 Documentation for 
\series bold
Mega2+R
\series default
 can be found at 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://watson.hgen.pitt.edu/register/docs/Mega2R.html
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

