\begin_layout Section
Background
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
During an association or linkage analysis project, one may need to analyze
 the data with several different programs.
 Unfortunately, it can often be quite difficult to get one's data in the
 proper format desired by each different computer program.
 Not only must the data be converted to the proper format, but also the
 loci must be reordered into their proper order.
 Writing custom reformatting scripts can be error-prone and very time-consuming.
 To address these problems, we created Mega2, which can be obtained from
 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://watson.hgen.pitt.edu/register/
\end_layout

\end_inset

 or via BitBucket 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://bitbucket.org/dweeks/mega2
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 The early Mega2 could read input data in a few formats: LINKAGE format
 and Mega2 table format.
 Mega2 augments these input formats with additional information.
 For example, trait locus penetrance information can be specified as well
 as the allele frequencies.
 Of course, the frequencies are calculated if not provided in a file.
 The earliest version of Mega2 was written in the C computer language.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Over time, Mega2 was upgraded to the C++ computer language and some of the
 internals were rewritten.
 In time, more linkage analysis programs were created (by others) and were
 added to Mega2.
 The data formats that GWAS data were provided in changed over time; now
 Mega2 can also read data in PLINK format, IMPUTE format and VCF format.
 The volume of GWAS marker data increased significantly, so Mega2 was extended
 to support the compression of biallelic genotype data (though still supporting
 multi-satellite and other non-compressed legacy data).
 As the volume of genotype data increased, it became slow and burdensome
 to validity check the genotype data and generate allele frequency data
 for each separate analysis that Mega2 was invoked for.
 Rather, Mega2 was restructured so that the validation and metadata generation
 was performed once for a given study and stored in a SQLite3 database file.
 Mega2 uses this database file, via a menu-driven interface, to provde the
 data needed for any particular analysis.
 The same database file serves as the input for all the different analyses.
 (Note: Mega2 is structured so that the user proceeds through a series of
 menus, both to create the database and later to process it.
 After the desired output files are created, Mega2 exits.
 It is not particularly interactive.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Sqlite3 Database
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Data frames are not a bad way at all to represent database tables.
 (??tidyverse) The earlier Mega2's C (language) structures were mapped into
 SQL database tables with some 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

glue
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 fields.
 There are tables for loci data, marker data, allele data, map data, phenotype
 data and genotype data as well as pedigree and person data.
 Most tables are composed of integer, numeric and string data.
 The genotype data are composed of multiple values with 8 bytes of data
 per value either representing a quantitative value or an affective value.
 The genotype data is a raw vector with a pair of bytes representing a genotype
 for non biallelic markers or can be a raw compressed bit vector with two
 bits per genotype.
 After the data frames are loaded from the database, you may select 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

observations
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 needed for your particular study.
 There is also a special function for extracting customary nucleotides from
 the genotype data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Of course, many other programming environments can process databases,
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

 in particular SQLite databases.
 Of particular interest is R because there is a wealth of existing genetic
 analysis programs already create, well documented, and available in both
 CRAN and Bioconductor.
 Also, researchers regularly use R to develop analysis because it has been
 used in the past to develop statistical programs and has the resultant
 libraries readily available.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rest of this document first shows some examples of package use.
 Then we discuss the main package, 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
.
 The several additional packages, 
\emph on
mega2pedgene, mega2vcf and mega2genabel
\emph default
 are presented which not only accomplish there named function but also illustrat
e examples of how the 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 package can be used by other packages.
 Note: the functions here are discussed at an overview level.
 If you need programming details, you should look at the CRAN package documentat
ion for the package of interest or look at the actual code.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Examples
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is always difficult to share real data.
 We have chosen to use the SeqSIMLA2 program to generate a dataset for us.
 [SeqSIMLA2: simulating correlated quantitative traits accounting for shared
 environmental effects in user-specified pedigree structure, Chung RH1,
 Tsai WY, Hsieh CH, Hung KY, Hsiung CA, Hauser ER., Genet Epidemiol.
 2015 Jan;39(1):20-4.
 doi: 10.1002/gepi.21850.
 Epub 2014 Sep 22.] Download the code from 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://sourceforge.net/projects/seqsimla/?source=typ_redirect
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 To make our results easy to follow, we started from the first example under
 the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

prevelance
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 tab on the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Simulate by Disease status
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 tab on the tutorial on the 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

http://seqsimla.nhri.org.tw/prevalence
\end_layout

\end_inset

page.
 This simulation creates 1380 samples of 500,000 markers; a bit too much
 for sharing.
 In addition, a bit less than 200,000 are actually polymorphic while the
 rest are monomorphic.
 We chose to subsample the latter down to 1380 people and 1000 markers.
 These data will be used to illustrate Mega2 and Mega2+R operations.
\end_layout

