Package Implementations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 Package Primitive Functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
 function and tables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 package function, 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
, reads the necessary tables from an SQLite database into a collection of
 data frames.
 It returns an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 containing these data.
 If the verbose flag is true, summary statistics will be printed as the
 data frames are created.
 Variables ending in 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\series bold
_table
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, viz.
 person_table, locus_table, are raw data directly copied from the database,
 although some of the original fields are not necessary and thus not copied.
 Other variables in the R environment are derived information.
 The 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 dataframe collects data from the 
\series bold
pedigree_table, person_table,
\emph on
 
\series default
\emph default
and 
\series bold
phenotype_table
\series default
 to produce the data frame equivalent of a PLINK .fam file.
 The 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table
\series default
 collects all the raw data bytes by chromosome from the 
\series bold
genotype_table
\series default
 for each person and makes a single new vector for each person.
 But the 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table 
\series default
can introduce up to three 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

virtual markers
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 at the end of each chromosome if the chromosome length is not an exact
 multiple of 4 (genotypes per byte).
 The 
\series bold
marker_table
\series default
 contains the offset of each marker in the genotype data vector assuming
 all the markers across all the chromosomes are contiguous.
 We construct the corresponding offset for the 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table
\series default
 and add it to the 
\series bold
marker_table
\series default
.
 The 
\series bold
marker
\series default
\emph on
 
\emph default
data frame is a subset of the 
\series bold
marker_table
\series default
 data containing the name, chromosome, position, original marker offset
 and marker offset for the 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table
\series default
.
 (Note: Creating the 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table
\series default
 with no 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

virtual markers
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 would require a lot of bit manipulations in R which would not be terribly
 efficient while keeping track of the gap is not particularly hard.
 This could all be done in Rcpp why use it if you can use R.
 Of course, the Mega2 program is written cpp and so does, in fact, just
 copy out the markers it needs.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Another feature worth noting is how we pull together the rows from the 
\series bold
pedigree_table, person_table,
\emph on
 
\series default
\emph default
and 
\series bold
phenotype_table
\series default
.
 The answer is simple: 
\series bold
pedigree_table
\series default
 has a pedigree_link
\series bold
, person_table
\series default
 has a pedigree_link and person_link
\series bold
,
\emph on
 
\series default
\emph default
and 
\series bold
phenotype_table
\series default
 has a person_link field.
 The data in each 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

_link
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 field is monotonic, unique and was created to capture memory pointers that
 could not be copied to the database.
 These link fields can be readily used by the R merge function operations
 to assemble data.
 As you might imagine, the 
\series bold
genotype_table
\series default
 also has a person_link field.
 The analog to these 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

_link
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 fields on the marker related data frames is the locus_link field.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
mkfam 
\emph default
and
\emph on
 setfam
\emph default
 functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 package function, 
\emph on
setfam
\emph default
, may be not necessary.
 The function, 
\emph on
mkfam
\emph default
, was called by default from 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
 to create the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 table.
 You might want to prune the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 table to restrict the samples processed.
 For, example you might want to eliminate samples with unavailable case/control
 status.
 After you prune the table, 
\emph on
setfam
\emph default
 will reset the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 table in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 It will also prune the 
\series bold
phenotype_table
\series default
 and 
\series bold
genotype_table
\series default
 in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to have only those persons left in the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 table.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
getgenotypesraw
\emph default
 functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now we get to the good stuff; its time to extract genotype data.
 We presume that we have adjusted the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 table as necessary.
 The functions, 
\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
getgenotypesraw
\emph default
 return a matrix of nucleotide pairs or encode integers with a row for each
 sample.
 They both take the same single argument which is a subset of the 
\series bold
marker
\series default
 data frame.
 The offsets are used by the internal code while the name, chromosome and
 position can be used to identify the data to the user.
 These two functions are really dispatches that call into Rcpp code.
 They call the proper function for the level of compression: biallelic and
 microsatellite.
 They also collect genotypes and alleles from appropriate 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 tables to pass as explicit arguments to the Rcpp code.
 For each marker, 
\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 will return the corresponding pair of nucleotides as a string.
 
\emph on
getgenotypesraw
\emph default
 will return an integer for the first allele and an integer for the second
 allele; the two integers are returned as a single value with the integer
 for the first allele shifted 16 bits and or'ed to the integer for the second
 allele.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We might have the genotype data to create a 1000's of samples by 1,0000,000's
 of markers matrix in R.
 But the stress this places on the machine will not be acceptable.
 Thus we introduced more functions to partition the set of markers into
 reasonable chunks.
 In particular, most probably we only wan t to process a chromosome or even
 some genes at a time; not all the markers we know about.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\emph on
mega2 
\emph default
Package Marker Range Functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
setGenes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If we can not readily process all the markers at once, what do we typically
 want to do is look at some genes.
 Presumably, we want to get all the genotypes associated with the markers
 of some collection of genes.
 We have imported the Bioconductor data Annotations: "org.Hs.eg.db"and "TxDb.Hsapien
s.UCSC.hg19.knownGene" into the 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 package.
 The former translates between gene name or alias and the entrezID of the
 gene.
 The latter gives start position, end position and chromosome for each transcrip
t known for the each gene.
 You might want to use the data from another build available at Bioconductor
 or follow their instructions make your own mapping.
 The 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 function 
\emph on
setGenes
\emph default
 lets you supply alternate string names for these data.
 Mega2 will load these Namespace from R when you access the 
\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 is the main interface to Mega2+R.
 You specify a list of genes to extract the transcripts for; then Mega2
 computes the list of markers that lie in each transcript.
 It computes an array of genotypes for each transcript from its markers
 and finally it invokes a call back function that was supplied as an argument
 to the 
\emph on
applyFntoGenes
\emph default
 function.
 The call back function will be called with three arguments: the first is
 a matrix of genotypes for all the markers in the transcript, the second
 is a data frame of the markers for the transcript, and the third is a single
 row from the data frame that defines the transcript (it has the at least
 names, ID, start position, end position, and chromosome).
 The call back function can apply whatever analysis is desired for the markers
 of the gene.
 It can also access the other data frames available in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 is intended to be the front end.
 By giving it specific named arguments, you can indicate the call back should
 be applied to each of several sets of markers in arbitrary ranges on chromosome
s.
 Or you can specify the entire chromosome to define the range of markers.
 And finally, you can just specify an arbitrary list of markers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
setRanges
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are several reasons that the specific officially provided ranges for
 the number and placement gene transcripts may not be to your liking.
 Some the transcripts for a gene appear to have the same start/end position
 of others but with a different transcript name.
 Some transcripts overlap others.
 The transcripts have one start and one end but do not account for non coding
 regions, promoters, etc.
 Perhaps you would prefer to specify names and for each name provide a start
 position, end position and chromosome to your liking.
 The 
\emph on
setRanges
\emph default
 function records a range data frame that has at least the start position,
 end position and chromosome 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

observations
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 It also records a vector of 3 integer that indicates which variable in
 the data frame contains the chromosome, start position and end position.
 These range and selector data are used as a default.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 has a call back function of three arguments just like 
\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
.
 With no additional arguments, it will iterate through each row of the default
 range, compute the contained markers and then invoke the call back.
 Alternatively, 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 may be given a range and selector argument which will cause it to use the
 ranges supplied rather than the default.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
applyFnToMarkers
\emph default
 function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
applyFnToMarkers
\emph default
 is the simplest function that uses the previously discussed call back function.
 It takes one additional argument that is a data frame of markers following
 the layout of the supplied 
\series bold
marker
\series default
 data frame.
 It creates a genotype matrix for these markers and then invokes the call
 back function with the genotype matrix, the marker data frame and NULL.
 (NULL because there is no range information.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\emph on
mega2pedgene
\emph default
 Package Functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The CRAN pedgene package, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Gene-Level Statistics for Pedigree Data
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, performs 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Gene-level association tests with disease status for pedigree data: kernel
 and burden association statistics
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 It would serve as a perfect test for 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 mechanism described earlier.
 This is especially true since we have the results of running this test
 on many genes for one of our internal test datasets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You first call 
\emph on
init_pedgene
\emph default
 rather than 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
; the former uses a function in the 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 package to load a Mega2 database.
 But it also has to modify the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 table to eliminate samples where the case/control is not know.
 In addition, it computes and saves some state that it will use later.
 
\emph on
run_pedgene
\emph default
 invokes 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 on the same ranges of data that our previous test used.
 In addition, the call back function, 
\emph on
DOpedgene
\emph default
, replicates the code that the previous test used with the genotype data
 slightly adjusted to the new format.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Yes the results produced are the same, but the original test was hand coded
 and hard to change.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\emph on
mega2vcf
\emph default
 Package Functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This package serves several purposes.
 It shows you how to assemble data from the Mega2 database into more conventiona
l usage.
 It provides a regression for Mega2+R by producing the same results from
 the SQLite database that Mega2 can produce.
 All the VCF related files should be the same.
 Finally, it allows VCF format files to be generated for R analysis that
 need this format input.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You must call the 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB 
\emph default
function from the 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 package to load a Mega2 database, before the functions in this package
 can be used.
 This package takes the data that are available in the Mega2 data frames
 and writes them out into several files.
 The .vcf file is the main output; it is created from the genotype matrix,
 and that data are supplemented by info from the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 table and 
\series bold
allele_table
\series default
 table.
 The 
\emph on
Mega2VCF
\emph default
 program performs this task.
 One interesting aspect of the code is how it deals with a potentially large
 numbers of markers.
 Simply it attacks the problem a chunk at a time, where a chunk contains
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

C
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 markers (currently 1000).
 It generates the genotype matrix and related info for the chunk then append
 the results for these markers to the .vcf output file.
 Then it reloads new data into the chunk and writes appends it again.
 This looping strategy might prove useful for other uses of the data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We also generate several more files:
