 The code in the provided functions illustrates how to assemble the correspondin
g data from the Mega2 database.
 The 
\emph on
mkVCFphe
\emph default
 is a bit subtle.
 The database contains a raw vector of 8 * the number of phenotypes byte
 (per person).
 The 8 bytes are either one double precision float number or two integers
 depending on an encoding in the 
\series bold
locus_table
\series default
.
 After selecting each 8 byte vector, 
\emph on
readBin
\emph default
 is used for converting the raw bits to the correct form.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\emph on
mega2genabel
\emph default
 Package Functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The GenABEL CRAN package provides may functions for GWAS analysis.
 It also accepts GWAS data in several convenient formats.
 But Mega2 accepts input in more formats, notably, PLINK bed, VCF, IMPUTE2
 and even linkage.
 Thus 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
 can be a bridge to easily convert your data into GenABEL format.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You must call the 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB 
\emph default
function from the 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
 package to load a Mega2 database, before the functions in this package
 can be used.
 The function, 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
, returns a gwaa.data-class object.
 It operates by writing the Mega2 database data into one of the formats
 that GenABEL supports; in this case PLINK tped format.
 This requires a .fam file, a .phe file and a .tped file.
 The latter looks very much like a VCF .vcf file with entries on each row
 for the genotypes of each sample.
 (There is less fields before the genotype data and the genotype data is
 show as real nucleotides rather than VCF REF/ALT field references.) 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
 calls the GenABEL 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

glue
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 functions to process the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

tped
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 file into a generic format and then to convert that format to a GenABEL
 object.
\end_layout

